---
// Methods: how the numbers are made. First section: the arena-scorer adjustment, with every arena's real factors.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import data from '../../public/data/arena_factors.json';
import fp from '../../public/data/fingerprints.json';
const events = [['hits', 'Hits'], ['gives', 'Giveaways'], ['takes', 'Takeaways'], ['blocks', 'Blocked shots']] as const;
type Row = (typeof data.arenas)[number] & Record<string, number | string>;
const arenas = (data.arenas as Row[]).filter((a) => (a.home_games as number) >= 82);
const pct = (f: number) => { const p = Math.round((f - 1) * 100); return p === 0 ? 'even' : `${p > 0 ? '+' : '−'}${Math.abs(p)}%`; };
const ex = [...arenas].sort((a, b) => (b.hits as number) - (a.hits as number))[0];
const sample = 30;
const hr = (data as any).home_road as Record<string, { home_rate: number; away_rate: number }>;
const settle = Object.entries(fp.stabilization as Record<string, { r: number; k_games: number }>).sort((a, b) => a[1].k_games - b[1].k_games);
const names: Record<string, string> = { volume: 'Shot volume', quality: 'Shot quality', rush: 'Rush offense', turnover: 'Off-turnover offense', point: 'Point-shot reliance', suppression: 'Shot suppression', qualityAllowed: 'Quality allowed', breakdowns: 'Breakdowns avoided', goalie: 'Goaltending', pace: 'Pace', forecheck: 'Forecheck pressure', physical: 'Physicality', rebounds: 'Second chances', pp: 'Power play', pk: 'Penalty kill', powerKill: 'Power kill', discipline: 'Discipline' };
---
<Base title="Methods" mock={false}>
  <h1 class="page-h">How the numbers are made</h1>
  <p class="lede">Plain-language explanations of the adjustments behind the site, with the real figures.</p>

  <Section id="arena" title="The arena adjustment" placeholder={false} takeaway="Hits, giveaways, takeaways and blocked shots are counted by people, and some buildings count more generously than others.">
    <div class="card explain">
      <p><strong>What it is.</strong> Every NHL arena has its own off-ice scorers. A bump along the boards might be a hit in one building and nothing in another. Left alone, that makes teams look more or less physical just because of where they play half their games.</p>
      <p><strong>How it’s calculated.</strong> For each arena, we take every game played there over three seasons ({data.seasons}) and work out how often each event is recorded per 60 minutes of play, for both teams combined. Then we do the same for that home team’s road games. Dividing the first by the second gives the arena’s factor. Because even three seasons is a limited sample, each factor is pulled a little of the way back toward 1.00.</p>
      <p><strong>A worked example.</strong> At {ex.venue}, scorers recorded <strong>{ex.hits_here}</strong> hits per 60. In {ex.team}’s road games, the same team and its opponents were credited with <strong>{ex.hits_elsewhere}</strong>. {ex.hits_here} ÷ {ex.hits_elsewhere} = <strong>{(ex.hits_raw as number).toFixed(2)}</strong>, which settles at <strong>{(ex.hits as number).toFixed(2)}</strong> after the pull toward 1.00. So a night with {sample} recorded hits there counts as <strong>{(sample / (ex.hits as number)).toFixed(1)}</strong>.</p>
      <p><strong>How it’s shown.</strong> We never display an adjusted count, because that would look like a number of hits that nobody recorded. Instead, arena-adjusted stats appear as a <em>score</em>, where 100 is league average. The “Hits and takeaways” switch at the top of every page flips to the raw numbers exactly as recorded.</p>
    </div>
    <h3 class="sub">Home and road</h3>
    <div class="card explain">
      <p>Separately from any one building, home teams are credited with more of these events everywhere in the league. Over three seasons, home teams were given <strong>{hr.hits.home_rate}</strong> hits per 60 to visitors’ <strong>{hr.hits.away_rate}</strong>, <strong>{hr.takes.home_rate}</strong> takeaways to <strong>{hr.takes.away_rate}</strong>, and also more giveaways: <strong>{hr.gives.home_rate}</strong> to <strong>{hr.gives.away_rate}</strong>.</p>
      <p>That could be scorers watching the home side more closely, or teams genuinely having the puck more at home; public data can’t separate the two. Either way, it would flatter a team in the middle of a long homestand. So events are weighted to put home and road games on the same footing, the same way shots are.</p>
      <p>We also tested whether particular buildings favour their home team more than others do. The differences were there in any single season but did not repeat from one season to the next, which means they are noise rather than a habit. Correcting for them would make the numbers worse, so we don’t.</p>
    </div>
    <h3 class="sub">Every arena’s factors</h3>
    <p class="note">How much more or less each building records than its home team’s road games do. “+19%” means 19% more.</p>
    <div class="card tablecard">
      <table class="at">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Arena</th><th scope="col">Home team</th>{events.map(([, l]) => <th scope="col">{l}</th>)}</tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {arenas.map((a) => (
            <tr><th scope="row">{a.venue}</th><td>{a.team}</td>{events.map(([k]) => <td data-tip={`${a[`${k}_here`]} per 60 here, ${a[`${k}_elsewhere`]} in ${a.team}’s road games. Factor ${(a[k] as number).toFixed(2)}.`}>{pct(a[k] as number)}</td>)}</tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">Arenas with fewer than two seasons of games in this window are left out of the table and treated as neutral until there is enough to judge.</p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="blend" title="Blending this season with last" placeholder={false} takeaway="Early in a season, a handful of games says little about some traits and a lot about others.">
    <div class="card explain">
      <p>With “This season and last” selected, each number is a mix of this season’s games and the team’s previous season. How much it leans on last season depends on the trait. We measured, from three past seasons, how many games it takes before a trait is as much signal as noise:</p>
      <ul class="settle">{settle.map(([k, v]) => <li><span>{names[k] ?? k}</span><strong>{v.k_games >= 300 ? 'over 300' : Math.round(v.k_games)} games</strong></li>)}</ul>
      <p>So shot volume is trustworthy within a couple of weeks, while goaltending and penalty killing stay close to league average until much later. “This season only” turns the blending off and shows the raw swing.</p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section id="score" title="Score and venue adjustment" placeholder={false} takeaway="Teams that are behind shoot more, and so do home teams. Shot-based numbers are corrected for both.">
    <div class="card explain">
      <p>A team protecting a lead sits back; a team chasing one throws everything at the net. Comparing raw shot totals would reward teams for trailing. Each 5-on-5 shot is therefore weighted by the score at the time and by whether the shooter is at home, so that across the league the home and away totals balance in every score situation.</p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section id="xg" title="How dangerous a chance is" placeholder={false} takeaway="Every shot gets a rating: how often shots like it go in.">
    <div class="card explain">
      <p>The site uses its own expected-goals model, trained on about 360,000 shots from three seasons. For each shot it looks at where it was taken from, what kind of shot it was, what happened just before (a save, a turnover, a faceoff) and how long ago, and the manpower on the ice. It deliberately doesn’t know who shot or who was in net.</p>
      <p>Tested on a full season it had never seen, a rating of 10% meant the puck went in 10.0% of the time. Because one model rates every season, numbers from different years can be compared fairly. <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}xg-check/`}>See how it rated the chances in recent games.</a></p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section id="lines" title="Numbering the lines" placeholder={false} takeaway="First line, second line and so on are decided by ice time, not reputation.">
    <div class="card explain">
      <p>A team’s lines and defense pairs are numbered by how much 5-on-5 ice time each group has played together over its last ten games. The group with the most is the first line. That means a line can be numbered lower than fans expect if its players have been shuffled between different linemates.</p>
      <p>Each line’s ratings compare it with the same-numbered line on the other 31 teams: first lines with first lines, third pairs with third pairs.</p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section id="per60" title="What “per 60” means" placeholder={false} takeaway="Most rates on the site are per 60 minutes of ice time.">
    <div class="card explain">
      <p>A top line plays far more than a fourth line, and a team with a lot of power plays gets far more power-play shots than one with few. To compare them fairly, almost every rate on the site is scaled to 60 minutes of ice time in that situation: “2.9 expected goals per 60” means what the group would produce in a full hour at its current pace. Ice time itself is written as minutes and seconds, like 9:15.</p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section id="limits" title="Blind spots" placeholder={false}>
    <ul class="limits">
      <li><strong>Passes before a shot</strong> aren’t in public data, so expected-goal numbers can’t see a cross-ice feed. Goalies behind teams that allow those look worse than they are.</li>
      <li><strong>Rush chances</strong> are identified from the timing of recorded events, which catches only some of them. Treat rush numbers as a way to compare teams, not a full count.</li>
      <li><strong>Defender positions</strong> aren’t tracked publicly, so “breakdowns” are inferred from dangerous rush and turnover chances.</li>
    </ul>
  </Section>
</Base>

<style>
  .page-h { font: 700 clamp(34px, 7vw, 52px) / 1.05 var(--display); letter-spacing: -0.02em; margin-top: 26px; }
  .lede { color: var(--ink-2); margin-top: 10px; font-size: 18px; }
  .explain { margin-top: 18px; display: grid; gap: 12px; }
  .explain p { font: 400 16px/1.6 var(--body); }
  .tablecard { overflow-x: auto; margin-top: 12px; }
  .at { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 14px; min-width: 620px; }
  .at th, .at td { text-align: right; padding: 8px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); white-space: nowrap; }
  .at thead th { font-size: 11px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.05em; color: var(--ink-3); }
  .at th:first-child, .at td:nth-child(2) { text-align: left; }
  .at tbody th { font-weight: 600; }
  .at tbody tr:last-child > * { border-bottom: 0; }
  .settle { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(min(100%, 280px), 1fr)); gap: 0 28px; font-family: var(--ui); }
  .settle li { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: 12px; padding: 7px 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); font-size: 14px; }
  .limits { margin-top: 16px; display: grid; gap: 10px; font: 400 15px/1.5 var(--ui); }
</style>
